BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:DesignTO Tours: Deacon Residence
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260708T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260708T190000
UID:blogto-236111@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/06/05/original_imageba542689-95bb-43f0-9eee-6e7561b4a0
 98.jpg
DESCRIPTION:As part of our charitable fundraising efforts\, join DesignTO 
 for an exclusive house tour in Toronto’s Little Italy neighbourhood.\nTh
 e remaking of this Toronto home was a collaboration between owner Tom Deac
 on and his longtime friend Andrew Jones\, both graduates of architecture a
 t the University of Toronto. Soon after graduating\, Deacon shifted his fo
 cus to furniture design\, going on to a successful career designing a broa
 d range of seating mostly for large scale production. Jones\, after adding
  a degree in furniture design from the Royal College of Art in London\, al
 so developed a career in furniture and product design while continuing to 
 practice architecture and interior design. This dual experience forms the 
 foundation of his work.\nOne starting point for their approach was a desir
 e to make a home for Tom’s collection of furniture and art\, inherited\,
  collected\, and made by Tom himself\, manipulating light\, space\, and ma
 terials to create a rich yet quiet series of spaces that would enhance the
  experience of the eclectic collection rather than compete with it.\nThe 
 “bones” of the project was a 4-storey\, 1893 brick Victorian house on 
 an historic street in Toronto’s Little Italy neighbourhood. Previous own
 ers had stripped all historic details\, inside and out so while Tom decide
 d on a meticulous recreation of the original facade using English brick\, 
 the closest match now available to the original Toronto red brick\, the in
 terior presented a “tabula rasa” for the designers who approached the 
 design meticulously.\nExperience this special space yourself by joining th
 e tour led by homeowner Tom Deacon\, designer Andrew Jones\, and DesignTO 
 Artistic Director Deborah Wang.\nThe exact address for the house tour will
  be shared with ticket holders. Please respect the privacy of the homeowne
 rs by keeping their address confidential.
LOCATION:Deacon Residence
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/designto-tours-deacon-residence-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Europro Presents NAC Courtyard Sessions
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260708T113000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260708T133000
UID:blogto-237090@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/06/26/original_image3be63ccf-dae7-4df4-afac-2e4bb328cd
 a1.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Europro Presents NAC Courtyard Sessions: Free Summer Live Musi
 c Comes to Yonge Street\nSix free Wednesday concerts transform the North A
 merican Centre courtyard into a vibrant cultural gathering space\, featuri
 ng emerging talent\, local favourites\, and award-winning Canadian musicia
 ns\nTORONTO\, ON — This summer\, Europro presents NAC Courtyard Sessions
 \, a free outdoor live music series running every Wednesday from July 8 to
  August 12\, 2026 at the North American Centre courtyard (5650 Yonge Stree
 t\, North York). Presented by Europro in partnership with the Emerging Art
 ists Association (EAA) and the Willowdale Arts Collective\, the series bri
 ngs curated lunch-hour and sunset performances to the heart of Yonge Stree
 t — featuring emerging artists\, local favourites\, and award-winning Ca
 nadian musicians. Admission is free and open to all.\nThe series unfolds i
 n two formats. The Lunch Jam (July 8\, 15\, and 22\, 11:30 a.m. – 1:30 p
 .m.) offers relaxed daytime performances featuring up-and-coming jazz\, so
 ul\, acoustic\, and crossover artists. The Sunset Series (July 29\, August
  5\, and 12\, 5:30 p.m. – 7:30 p.m.) delivers higher-energy evening perf
 ormances by notable artists\, paired with curated social experiences desig
 ned for after-work community gathering.\n“NAC Courtyard Sessions is abou
 t bringing live music into the everyday rhythm of the city\,” says David
  M.J. Lee\, Artistic Director of the Summer Live Music Festival. “Whethe
 r it’s a midday break or the end of a workday\, we want people to step i
 nto the courtyard\, hear extraordinary Canadian talent\, and feel part of 
 a community — all for free.”
LOCATION:North American Centre
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/europro-presents-nac-courtyard-sessions-
 toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Football Fandom: Community or Commodity\, A DOHA DEBATES TOWN HALL
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260708T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260708T210000
UID:blogto-237421@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/07/04/original_image44274f28-bc32-46cc-9689-6e72a4981f
 72.jpg
DESCRIPTION:The Terminal Theatre is pleased to welcome the Doha Debates to
  our theatre with their town hall event Football fandom: Community or comm
 odity?\nOn July 8\, international experts will join students and recent gr
 aduates from Qatar\, Canada and Mexico to debate the question: What happen
 s to football fandom when big money gets involved?\nWith our city currentl
 y hosting the World Cup\, soccer (football) is at the forefront of our col
 lective consciousness. Football fandom: Community or commodity? will be a 
 fascinating discussion about how the sport has changed.\nGlobally speaking
 \, twenty years ago\, the football club you followed usually offered insig
 ht into where you came from. Today\, Manchester United trades on the New Y
 ork Stock Exchange and counts its followers in the hundreds of millions\, 
 most of them Asian and African nationals. Still\, they cry when United los
 es. They fight in the comment sections. The sense of belonging is real—b
 ut what exactly do these fans belong to?\nModerator Imran Garda will be jo
 ined by Shireen Ahmed\, a senior contributor at CBC Sports and co-owner of
  Lewes FC in East Sussex\, England\; David Goldblatt\, a writer\, historia
 n and sociologist of football\; and Kely Nascimento\, president of Nascime
 nto Foundation and co-founder of Hadaf Global\, to discuss how football’
 s transformation into a global business is reshaping the meaning of the ga
 me and its fandom.\nAs more clubs are purchased by investors with little c
 onnection to the communities they represent\, how are the bonds between su
 pporters and their teams impacted? Is the commercialization of football cr
 eating a more inclusive and connected game\, or turning loyalty into just 
 another product to be bought and sold? And when fans are separated from th
 eir clubs by oceans\, cultures\, and continents\, what does it really mean
  to belong?\nBroadcast around the world on Aljazeera TV and presented by t
 he Qatar Foundation in partnership with Qatar\, Canada and Mexico 2026 Yea
 r of Culture. The Doha Debates are free to attend.\nFREE\nTicket Required 
 for Entry\nAge Restrictions: Must be over 15 years of age to attend event.
LOCATION:The Terminal Theatre
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/football-fandom-community-or-commodity-a
 -doha-debates-town-hall-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
