BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Making Waves at The Beach
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260726T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260726T123000
UID:blogto-236697@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/06/17/original_image75702a0c-5b8a-4ba5-9678-9cd7971891
 d8.jpg
DESCRIPTION:With toes in the sand\, join us as we stroll along the lakefro
 nt of this trendy Toronto neighbourhood defined by four beaches.\nBefore t
 he 1950s\, Torontonians took steamers and streetcars to escape the city to
  this once marshy cottage area in the east end. On this walk\, we’ll div
 e into its history\, from the shoreline engineering that created the sandy
  beach today\, to former amusement parks like Scarboro Beach Park known fo
 r its star attractions – the Tickler\, Tunnel of Love\, and Chute-the-Ch
 utes – and as the location of the first public exhibition flights of air
 planes in Canada.\nA playground for city residents\, this leisure area fea
 tured public parks like Kew Gardens which offered camping\, picnicking\, a
 nd amusements for tourists\, and the Balmy Beach Club\, a hub for sports l
 ike rugby and canoeing which spurred a legacy of athletic excellence.\nHid
 den beneath the water’s surface\, we’ll also expose the dark history o
 f the formation of the Swastika Club in the summer of 1933\, when a group 
 of Anglo-Canadian Nazi sympathizers harassed and attempted to drive  “un
 desirables” – recent immigrants but mainly Jewish bathers – away fro
 m local beaches.\nA roller coaster ride awaits you on this tour.
LOCATION:Donald D Summerville Olympic Pool
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/making-waves-at-the-beach-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
