BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Tiny Houses of Faith Walking Tour
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260726T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260726T163000
UID:blogto-237341@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/07/02/original_image01a5df89-82db-441c-af9e-ab1b3ba638
 35.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Look beyond the storefronts\, houses\, and former community ha
 lls of Leslieville\, and you’ll uncover a hidden landscape of faith.\nJo
 urney through Leslieville and discover how places of worship are tucked in
 to repurposed buildings\, revealing how these small\, everyday spaces have
  become community anchors providing spiritual and religious connection. Hi
 dden in former theatres\, storefronts\, halls\, and industrial buildings a
 re stories of migration\, adaptation\, and faith that reveal a different s
 ide of Toronto’s history.\nAlong the way\, we’ll visit sites including
  Fatih Mosque\, a former Orange Order hall\; Hoa Nghiem Temple\, a repurpo
 sed warehouse built by Toronto’s Vietnamese Buddhist community\; the Tur
 kish Canadian Islamic Trust\, housed in a former cinema and lesbian bar\; 
 and Shromani Sikh Sangat\, Toronto’s first gurdwara\, established in a f
 ormer grain store. Together\, we’ll explore how communities from Turkey\
 , Vietnam\, Pakistan\, Greece\, and India found ways to create spiritual h
 omes in the city.\nThis emerging historian tour will be led by Ridwan Bash
 ir\, a Master’s student studying History at Western University. Her rese
 arch focuses on migration and ethnic relations\, examining how immigrant c
 ommunities repurpose urban spaces to build community life in Canada.
LOCATION:Fatih Mosque
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/tiny-houses-of-faith-walking-tour-toront
 o/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
