BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Wild Mushroom Identification & Ecology with Stephan Lukacic in Tor
 onto's High Park
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260815T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260815T160000
UID:blogto-237035@blogto.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://blogto-production2-baselayer-display.blo
 gto.com/events/2026/06/25/original_imagea252bfff-0884-4925-8c11-0cb5e3a6b8
 05.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Popular outdoor educator and avid forager Stephan Lukacic take
 s you deep into science\, ecology\, and methodology of modern mushroom hun
 ting.\nThere are thousands of mushroom species in eastern North America. S
 ome have strange alien-looking shapes that don’t resemble the mushrooms 
 you find in the grocery store at all. Some look like heads of cauliflower\
 , some like beautiful corals you’d find under the sea\, others like socc
 er balls\, bird’s feathers\, or dangling icicles that hang from dead stu
 mps and logs\, or that burst up from the forest floor through leaves and d
 irt.\nWild mushrooms have curious folk names like “Dead Man’s Fingers
 ”\, “Shaggy Mane”\, “Witch’s Butter”\, and “Bear’s Head To
 oth”\; but their scientific names are far more accurate and important. S
 ome turn colours when they’re cut or bruised\, others smell like common 
 fruits\, taste acrid or bitter\, or even glow in the dark at night. Many w
 ild mushrooms are delicious\; others are toxic. Most are unpalatable\, fla
 vourless\, or too tough to eat. A few are even deadly. But how do you know
  which is which?\nThis short\, intensive class is designed to demystify wi
 ld mushrooms\, giving you a solid foundation on which to build future iden
 tification and foraging skills. The workshop begins with an engaging 90 mi
 n seminar. You will receive a 6-page handout covering all the basics of mu
 shroom hunting methodology\, and read and discuss its contents thoroughly.
  Then you’ll do a deep dive right into the forested areas of High Park
 —putting the knowledge you’ve acquired directly into practice by ident
 ifying wild mushrooms the group encounters in their natural habitat.\nMush
 room hunting is a fascinating\, gratifying\, and often misunderstood outdo
 or activity. Come learn the basics of these fungal wonders of nature\, lea
 rn tricks of the forager’s trade\, and have a blast doing it!\nNote: You
  will not be foraging for wild mushrooms to take home. This workshop focus
 es on teaching you how to find and identify wild edible mushrooms.\nPrice:
  $73\nAges: 18+ years\nMeeting Location: High Park Forest School\, 375 Col
 borne Lodge Drive\nPlease bring:\nA pen or pencil\nA notebook\nWeather-app
 ropriate clothing\nCuriosity\nReadiness for outdoor learning
LOCATION:High Park Nature Centre
URL:https://www.blogto.com/events/wild-mushroom-identification-ecology-wit
 h-stephan-lukacic-in-torontos-high-park-toronto/
X-SOURCE:blogTO
END:VEVENT
